<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Nalu — The Changeset Wave

//nalu// — Hawaiian: wave, surf; the wave-form that carries energy across distance.
A nalu does not carry one molecule of water — it carries the entire shaped movement
of the sea. The wave arrives as one coherent thing, composed of many.

The nalu names the changeset delivery pulse — the moment when scattered writes become
one consistent observed state delivered to all observers atomically. It is the
architectural invariant below the grammar layer. Operators do not emit nalu; the
engine delivers it.

''Source authority:'' Talk Story session 2026-05-15; Book of Verse ch. 14;
TW5 SyncAdaptor contract; Elm Architecture (Czaplicki 2012); UEFN SceneGraph Beta.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-wave-shape`>>

!! The Wave Shape — Three Implementations, One Pattern

The nalu appears as the same architectural shape in three layers of the Lararium stack:

```
TW5 layer:
  Multiple wiki.addTiddler() calls    ← scattered writes accumulate
  wiki.enqueueTiddlerEvent(title) ×N  ← change signals queued
  wiki.nextTick(callback)             ← batch boundary: JS call stack empties
  refresh(changedTiddlers)            ← WAVE ARRIVES: all widgets see same state

Verse / UEFN SceneGraph layer:
  Devices fire events, state mutates  ← scattered writes accumulate
  Engine batches between frames       ← batch boundary: simulation tick
  OnSimulate(StagedUpdates)<suspends> ← WAVE ARRIVES: all components see same updates

Lararium wiki shore (the landing queue) + nalu engine (the in-wiki applier):
  CRDT merge patches arrive           ← scattered writes (from peers, from CRDT ops)
  AutomergeDocStore → MemeProvider → IslandAdaptor.onChangeset → the landing queue, one family per grain
  host-task yield                     ← pacing boundary: one queue across ALL bags, whole families up to 200 landings between yields
  $tw.lares.applyNalu(change) × N     ← each change lands in the wiki, synchronously
  refresh(changedTiddlers)            ← WAVE ARRIVES: TW5's own nextTick dispatch carries what landed
```

''Unified-nalu law:'' ONE landing queue per wiki collects the changes of every CRDT bag in
arrival order. The wiki shore owns that queue and drains it in slices of whole families, up to 200
changes, yielding to the host between slices. ''The grain = one store change per dispatch'': a store
change carries a carrier's root, its children and its orphans' tombstones together, the provider hands
a shore the union of whole change events as one array, and the queue never cuts inside it (a family
larger than 200 lands whole in a slice of its own), so the wiki dispatches a root and its children
together and the disk projector never renders half a carrier; the nalu engine lands each change synchronously
(`$tw.lares.applyNalu`) and holds no queue, no timer and no frame. TW5 gathers changed titles
until its own scheduled `nextTick` dispatch, so the batch boundary belongs to the wiki and follows
no slice of the shore; the TiddlyWiki core ships no `wiki.transact()` and nothing on this surface calls one. Prior art
(Vue 3 scheduler, MobX `transaction`, Yjs `transact`, Solid `batch`, React 18 automatic
batching, S.js tick, the DREAM glitch-freedom paper) all converge on this shape: one
boundary per tick across heterogeneous sources beats N boundaries with the cascade-refresh
+ FRP-glitch anti-pattern. Our widget filter is O(1) hash-lookup, so the cost scales with
//changed// hashes, not bag count — no fan-out objection applies.

''A second nalu instance — local capture'': the same collect-then-flush
shape drives forward-facing capture, in a SEPARATE local ''non-federated'' worker-VM.
Producers (parallel sessions · worker swarms · Codex/Claude/Copilot) enqueue
born-annotated records into one unified queue; the ''server tick'' (the daemon's 20 Hz
`setInterval` lar-event-bus loop — the `LarTickCounter`, server-side where the palace
lives) crests, backpressure-gated; one drain files the batch to the mempalace web2
sidecar via the RFC-002 source-adapter. Two nalu engines, one pattern, ''two cadences'':
the ''federated'' in-wiki nalu reaches its observers on TW5's `nextTick` dispatch, its landing paced by host task (CRDT waves → the widget
shore); the ''local capture'' nalu crests on the server tick (CAPTURE waves → the palace
— lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/capture-annotation-model#/forward-facing-nalu). Do
not conflate their boundaries: the `nextTick` dispatch and the host-task pacing ride the wiki shore, the 20 Hz server
tick rides the local/daemon engine.

''The capture nalu rides daemon-plane as an idempotent cap'': the `#has capture` cap rides the
''@daemon'' (the bound-operator's vessel-only worker, severed from federation). Every daemon-plane
CARRIES the cap (tending the operator's session-capture is a daemon duty); its sink (the
`mine --source ndjson` flush) and feed (the producers) may be unwired, and that inert state is a
valid resting point — `role = capability ≠ platform`, the cap distinct so the FLOW capture never
gates the daemon's AUTHORITY dispatch (the master cut holds at the cap shore). The main thread
carries no data-plane. And every gate ''breathes'': the threshold self-regulates per family —
accumulate shrinks its batch depth under load, a timed coalesce GROWS its
window, the disk reconcile gate holds no window at all, the display coalesce stays frame-pinned (the projection-nalu #servo + #cells law: role =
physics ≠ uniformity).

''The home split:'' the ''federated in-wiki nalu'' (paced by host task, CRDT waves → the widget
shore) and the ''CRDT⟷TW5 projection-nalu'' stay ''sovereign TS'' — a first-class TS home, where much of
the projection tech lives. The ''capture / stream nalu-gate'' re-homes ''py-side'': the stream sensorium's
gate runs in py/R per-stream, and the TS coordinator drives it — feeding the py gate, consuming its crests.
The wave shape holds identically across the TS/py shore — collect-then-flush on either bank, the
substrate the water, the nalu the pattern.

''The invariant:'' The nalu is not one tiddler change. It is the atomic delivery
of a //batch// of changes to all observers simultaneously. Every observer — every
widget, every reaction handler — receives the same `changedTiddlers` set in the
same synchronous execution context. Within one dispatch no observer sees a partial state. A long wave crosses several
dispatches: the shore lands it slice by slice, and each dispatch hands every observer one whole set.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/below-the-grammar`>>

!! Below the Grammar Layer

The nalu is ''not'' a sigil the operator emits. It is what the engine delivers.

```
Grammar layer (above nalu):
  <<~ lele to=`lar:///target`>>    ← sigil declares intent
  <<~ hui>>                   ← sigil declares sync shape
  <<~ kukali trigger=`trigger`>>        ← sigil declares suspension

Nalu layer (below grammar):
  refresh(changedTiddlers)     ← engine delivers consistent state
  OnSimulate(StagedUpdates)    ← engine delivers consistent state
  [no sigil; no operator action; the engine acts]
```

The sigils in the grammar layer express //what should happen// — relationships,
concurrency shapes, reactions. The nalu is //when consistent state arrives// — the
moment all of those relationships become observable.

''The `\tick` sigil (when it lands)'' will live at the grammar layer — it will be
a device lifecycle declaration that says "run this procedure every nalu." The nalu
itself has no sigil; the `\tick` sigil hooks into the nalu.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/yin-collapse-law`>>

!! Yin-Collapse Law

''Law:'' The TW5 wiki (`$tw.wiki`) serves as the primary synchronous reactive engine.
The TS layer forms the minimal shore around it — only what TW5 cannot do from inside.

''The architecture:''

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  TW5: $tw.wiki             ← primary reactive engine │
│  TW5: Widget tree          ← refresh = nalu delivery │
│  TW5: nalu-engine          ← applier: one change     │
│  TW5: reaction-router      ← routing as startup code │
│  TW5: SharktoothSigil      ← grammar as tiddlers     │
│  TW5: Reaction tiddlers    ← bindings as tiddler data│
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  TS:  IslandAdaptor        ← wiki shore: queue+ledger│
│  TS:  AutomergeDocStore    ← Automerge CRDT          │
│  TS:  CompositeStore       ← recipe layer stack       │
│  TS:  VesselIslandPool     ← multi-island coordination│
│  TS:  Keyhive / network    ← crypto + peers          │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

''Inside the wiki (`module-type: startup`):''
* `nalu-engine` — the applier: `$tw.lares.applyNalu(change)` lands ONE change synchronously (a
  tombstone or an absent record deletes the title; anything else adds the tiddler, stamped
  `$origin-bag` from the envelope), and `$tw.lares.isApplyingNalu()` reads true for the length of
  that call. It holds no queue: the wiki shore installs its own door at `$tw.lares.enqueueNalu`
* `reaction-router` — `wiki.addEventListener("change")` listener that fires `tm-verse-event`
  for `lar:` tiddlers whose papalohe bindings name a listenable

''TS-side shore (irreducible — TW5 cannot do these):''
* `AutomergeDocStore` — CRDT, patch subscription, MemeProvider fan-out
* `IslandAdaptor` — the wiki shore: the landing queue and its door, the landed ledger (what each
  wave left under each title), the per-title hand gate, the draft book, and the outbound routing of a
  hand's save and delete by the composition's own layer order
* Network / WebRTC / WebSocket — browser/Node APIs
* Keyhive capabilities — cryptographic operations
* `VesselIslandPool` — multi-island coordination at the vessel layer

''The prior art validating this collapse:''
* ''Elm Architecture'' — one `Model`, one `update` loop, side effects as data (`Cmd`)
  — the wiki stands as the model; the wiki shore's landing produces tiddler writes; TW5 serves as the runtime
* ''Vue 3 reactive scheduler'' — reactive effects queue, flush on nextTick atomically
  — TW5 `enqueueTiddlerEvent` + `nextTick` + `refresh` is the same pattern
* ''MobX transaction/reaction'' — `transaction(() => {})` batches mutations, defers
  reactions until batch closes — TW5's nalu IS the MobX transaction close event

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/one-graph`>>

!! One Graph, Not Two

The causal islands are bags. Bags stack via TW5 recipe. The TW5 wiki is the
unified logical graph. CRDT provides per-bag physical federation.

```
One wiki graph (logical):
  TW5 recipe = [bag-A, bag-B, bag-C, ...]
  $tw.wiki sees all tiddlers from all bags as one namespace
  filter queries span all bags
  refresh(changedTiddlers) fires once for changes from any bag

Per-bag physical federation (Automerge):
  Each bag = one Automerge document
  CRDT merges happen per-document
  The wiki shore lands each bag's CRDT state → TW5 wiki tiddlers
  Nalu fires when TW5 dispatches the titles its nextTick queue gathered
```

''NOT two parallel reactive engines.'' The CRDT layer is the distributed persistent
store. TW5 serves as the live reactive view. The wiki shore queues CRDT change envelopes and the
nalu engine lands each one in the wiki; TW5's own dispatch delivers them as the wave.

```
CRDT (the sea)
  → AutomergeDocStore / MemeProvider (the wind shaping water)
    → IslandAdaptor.onUriChanged (every envelope joins the landing queue)
      → the landing queue (the swell builds across bags)
        → a host-task slice (the crest forms; the composition resolves each title)
          → $tw.lares.applyNalu(change) (the wave arrives on shore)
```

The widget tree receives the wave. One wiki shore stands per engine: every bag's changes enter it, and TW5 notifies the widgets when a landing changes what the wiki holds.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/tw5-scale-note`>>

!! TW5 Unfiltered Delivery — Scale Note

TW5's `refresh(changedTiddlers)` delivers to ALL widgets in the tree, unfiltered.
Each widget checks `changedTiddlers` via hash lookup (O(1) per widget).

''Performance profile:'' 10 VMs × 60 CRDT patches/second × 500 widgets =
300,000 hash lookups/second. This is manageable. Widget render cost (filter
expressions, wikitext) dominates, not the delivery lookup.

''Optimization path (without TW5 core rewrite):'' Widget filter expressions
that gate on `tiddler.bag === "bagX"` are hash lookups on a tiddler field.
Fast by default. No per-bag scoping needed in TW5's core refresh cycle.

''When a TW5 core extension becomes necessary:'' If VmPool scale reaches
100+ VMs with 1000+ widgets each, or if CRDT patch frequency exceeds 600/second
per VM, re-evaluate. The Stranger's concern (unfiltered delivery at scale) is
real but not yet triggered at current projected scale.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/uefn-alignment`>>

!! UEFN SceneGraph Alignment (Beta, June 2025)

The SceneGraph Beta unifies editor and runtime — all scene elements are now
Verse-addressable. `OnSimulate(StagedUpdates)<suspends>` is the canonical entry
point for per-tick logic across the full unified scene.

''Nalu ↔ OnSimulate mapping (confirmed):''

| TW5 concept | Verse concept | Lararium concept |
|---|---|---|
| `wiki.enqueueTiddlerEvent()` | Event fires during frame | CRDT patch arrives |
| `wiki.nextTick(cb)` | End-of-frame barrier | TW5's change-dispatch boundary, apart from the shore's slice quota |
| `refresh(changedTiddlers)` | `OnSimulate(StagedUpdates)` | ''nalu'' |
| `changedTiddlers` map | `StagedUpdates` array | changeset batch |
| Widget `refresh()` method | Device `OnSimulate()` method | per-observer delivery |

''Coordinate system note (v36.00):'' UEFN transitioned to LUF (Left, Up, Forward)
coordinates. Any `spatial:contains`, `spatial:adjacent`, or `spatial:portal` pranala
edges encoded with FRU orientation assumptions need migration. See `uefn-scene.md`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-surface`>>

!! The surface ~ one carrier per concept

The nalu surface spans a law, the gate families, one engine and two shores. Each concept keeps one
home; every other carrier on the surface points back here.

| Concept | Home | What it holds |
|---|---|---|
| the law | this carrier | the wave as changeset delivery, below the grammar; one graph, one shore per engine |
| the governor | `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/servo` | a bound that tunes itself from an observed signal |
| the gate families | `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/projection-nalu` | accumulate and coalesce, their guards, the level-triggered reconcile queue |
| the engine | `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/nalu-engine` | the in-wiki applier: one change, landed synchronously |
| the Automerge shore | `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/docs/meme-provider` | the fan-out between a document and its projections |
| the wiki shore, its law | `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/outbound-bridge` | a tiddler replaces a tiddler; the landed ledger, the base, the pacing gate, the discrete acts |
| the wiki shore, its adaptor | `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/island-adaptor` · `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/save-path` · `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/tw5/modules/sync-adaptor` | the adaptor's invariants, the outbound save path, the file's own account |
| the layers a hand lands in | `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/recipe-layer-model` | the composition's order, the working layer, the routing table |
| the disk shore | `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection` · `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/node/disk-projector` | the projection law and the node hand that enacts it |
| the roundtable | `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/syncer-seams-roundtable` | the syncer seams as talked through, kept as testimony |

''No clock decides on this surface.'' A timer may pace a hand at the island's edge; what leaves, what
lands and what refuses follow from state alone (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/no-global-now`).

